      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}+3?$",
      "worked_solution": "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199494/assets/75bb797ddbea41de97c1c4ba52e142342bfffb4f9b2b9b82111ebc94abf55446.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199494/assets/d68957038bfad7dfb4547d69abd178b087dde6c0f7c8ba95aa0fcd7547ccebe6.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{x}+3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,5)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14199494/assets/fb918b3042ca95c9401f2938258b7f13c909a87d18448c6e36c410a3d092dd94.png)",
